2.text:
On the $q\mapsto -q$ even part

\begin{equation*}
C_{E}:y^{2}=2E-q^{2}+q^{n},\qquad 
T_{n}(E)=\oint\frac{dq}{y},
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
u_{r}(n)=\Big[\frac{n-1}{2}\Big],\qquad 
u_{r}(2k)=k-1=\frac n2-1,\qquad 
u_{r}(2k+1)=2k=n-1 ,
\end{equation*}

so that $u_{r}(n)=n/2-1$ is the even case and $u_{r}(n\pm1)=n\pm1-1$
is the two odd neighbours.  Apart from the free $n=2$ the first
non–trivial reducible even exponents are

\begin{equation*}
n_{1}=4,\qquad n_{2}=6,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
u_{r}(4)=1,\;u_{r}(3)=2,\;u_{r}(5)=4,\qquad 
u_{r}(6)=2,\;u_{r}(7)=6,
\end{equation*}

and with the notation of the statement

\begin{equation*}
\alpha_{0}=\frac{n_{1}-1}{n_{2}}=\frac{3}{6}=\frac12 ,\qquad 
m=\frac{n_{1}}2=2,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\lambda_{12}=\frac{2}{n_{1}}\sqrt{\frac{n_{2}-n_{1}}{n_{1}/2}}
      =\frac{2}{4}\sqrt{\frac{2}{2}}=\frac12 .
\end{equation*}

On the even subspace

\begin{equation*}
H_{12}(p,q)=\frac12\Big[p^{2}+q^{2}-\lambda_{12}q^{m}\Big]
          =\frac{p^{2}}2+\frac{1-\lambda_{12}}2q^{2}
          =\frac{p^{2}}2+\frac{\omega_{12}^{2}}2q^{2},
\end{equation*}

i.e. the quadratic degenerate member of $H_{\mu}=p^{2}/2+q^{2}/2-
\mu q^{m}/2$ with

\begin{equation*}
\omega_{12}^{2}=1-\lambda_{12}=1-\frac12=\frac12,
\qquad \omega_{12}=2^{-1/2},
\qquad H_{12}=\frac{p^{2}}2+\frac{q^{2}}4 .
\end{equation*}

The Gauss–Manin period is $E$–independent.  In the mechanical
$2\pi$-normalisation

\begin{equation*}
T_{\rm phys}(E)=\oint\frac{dq}
{\sqrt{\,2E-\omega_{12}^{2}q^{2}\,}}
       =\frac{2\pi}{\omega_{12}}
       =\frac{2\pi}{\sqrt{\,1-\lambda_{12}\,}}
       =2\sqrt2\,\pi ,
\end{equation*}

so that $J(E)=E/\omega_{12}$ is linear.  In particular the $4\!-\!6$
even member is the ordinary harmonic oscillator
$H_{12}=p^{2}/2+q^{2}/4$ with the constant $E$-independent period
$2\pi/\omega_{12}$.

With the usual mathematics normalisation $T_{0}(0)=\pi$,

\begin{equation*}
T_{0}(\alpha_{0})=\frac{\pi}{\omega_{12}}=\pi\sqrt{\,1-\lambda_{12}\,
}^{-1}=\pi\sqrt2,
\end{equation*}

and with the $T_{0}\mapsto T_{0}/2\pi$ normalisation
$T_{\rm norm}(\alpha_{0})=\omega_{12}^{-1}=\sqrt2=(1-\lambda_{12})^{-1/2}$.
In the customary ${}_{2}F_{1}$ normalisation
$T_{\rm can}(\alpha)=\pi/\sin\pi\alpha$ this is the familiar constant

\begin{equation*}
T_{\rm can}\!\left(\alpha_{0}\right)=T_{\rm can}\!\left(\tfrac12\right)
      =\frac{\pi}{\sin\pi/2}=\pi,
\end{equation*}

i.e. $T_{\rm phys}=2T_{0}=2\pi\sqrt2$ and $T_{\rm norm}=\sqrt2$ up to
the ubiquitous $2\pi$ factor.  This is the $n_{1}=4,n_{2}=6$,
$\alpha_{0}=1/2$ reducible oscillator, the first non-trivial even
member of the family $u_{r}(2k)=k-1$, $u_{r}(2k\pm1)=2k\pm1-1$, $k\ge2$.
The next even member of the same family is $n_{1}=6,n_{2}=12$,
$\alpha=5/12$, etc. ∎

